#lang saffron
;; proper-tail-calls T6 (docs/archive/proper-tail-calls-plan.md, T-D6): a
;; dynamic call in tail position -- a call through a function VALUE, where no
;; callee is known at compile time -- runs in constant stack.
;;
;; Before T6 each such call was a nested C call through the callee's
;; direct-entry wrapper, about 285 bytes of stack a level (plan section 1):
;; ~30,000 deep at -O2, and far less at -O0.  Now a tail dynamic call either
;; BOUNCES -- records the call and returns to a trampoline driver one frame
;; below -- when the activation it sits in was entered from a driver, or
;; DRIVES the call in a loop otherwise.  The `flags` file builds this at -O0
;; (`--debug`), so the only thing standing between each loop and a stack
;; overflow is that trampoline.
;;
;; Five loops, one per way a dynamic tail call is reached:
;;
;;   run        -- a top-level function looping through itself as a value; it
;;                 is CPS-lowered (a dynamic call colors its caller), so this
;;                 is the `__cps` body bouncing to its direct-entry wrapper.
;;   loop       -- a capture-free lambda, lifted and CPS-lowered the same way.
;;   counter    -- a CAPTURING closure, emitted direct-style: its thunk is the
;;                 registered bouncer, and `__tb_may` is its permission.
;;   ping/pong  -- two different functions bouncing to each other, which is
;;                 the case neither a backedge (T3) nor fusion (T5) can reach.
;;   folded     -- a typed stdlib `vec-fold` invoking a Saffron callback whose
;;                 own tail is a dynamic call.  A typed caller never arms the
;;                 callee, so it must get a value back, never a bounce.
;;
;; `^tailcall` on each tail dynamic call is the assertion (T6 lets it annotate
;; a dynamic call; before, it had to be a static one).
;;
;; expected.timeout is 60 because this is SLOW ON PURPOSE, and the slowness is
;; not a finding.  ~4e6 dynamic tail calls at -O0 took 9 s on the ubuntu JIT
;; leg and 6 s on macOS (2026-10-03), i.e. ~2 us a call, against the 15 s
;; harness default -- 60% of budget on a leg whose suite wall clock spans 2.8x
;; run to run, so a slow draw would have killed it the way one killed
;; r7rs-tail-calls twice.
;;
;; That cost is MIR's JIT codegen, not the trampoline -- and not the -O0
;; either.  `tur jit` and `tur jit --debug` measure indistinguishable on the
;; same program, so the `flags` file's -O0 moves the COMPILED run and leaves
;; the JIT one alone.  Compiled at -O2 the same mechanism is 47 ns a call --
;; 1e7 dynamic calls in 471 ms, and a registry HIT is 13 ns
;; (benchmarks/saffron-dyn-tail-results.md, where T6 is a 2.7x SAVING over
;; the nested-call shape it replaced).
;;
;; The -O0 is still deliberate for the compiled run: at -O2 the optimizer
;; collapses these loops and the fixture stops proving constant stack at all.
;; So do not read this fixture's wall clock as a per-call cost, and do not
;; "fix" it by raising the optimization level -- that deletes the assertion.
;;
;; The JIT-vs-compiled RATIO is not established: the 47 ns baseline is
;; x86_64 Linux and the JIT figures so far came off a contended laptop, so
;; they mix machines and load.  A controlled measurement is follow-up work.
;;
;; run-jit.sh's summary prints the fixtures closest to their budget, so if
;; this one creeps back up it will show there before it fails.

(defn run [f n acc]
  (if (= n 0) acc ^tailcall (f f (- n 1) (+ acc 1))))

(defn make-counter [step]
  (fn [self n acc] (if (= n 0) acc ^tailcall (self self (- n 1) (+ acc step)))))

(defn ping [me other n]
  (if (= n 0) "ping" ^tailcall (other other me (- n 1))))

(defn pong [me other n]
  (if (= n 0) "pong" ^tailcall (other other me (- n 1))))

(defn apply-to [g x] ^tailcall (g x))

(defn step [acc x] (apply-to (fn [y] (+ y x)) acc))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (run run 1000000 0))
  (let [loop (fn [self n] (if (= n 0) "done" ^tailcall (self self (- n 1))))]
    (println (loop loop 1000000)))
  (let [counter (make-counter 3)]
    (println (counter counter 1000000 0)))
  (println (ping ping pong 1000001))
  (println (vec-fold [1 2 3 4 5] 100 step))
  0)
